    github.com/inconshreveable/mousetrap  Apache-2.0
                                          (indirect, reached through cobra, and
                                          linked only in a Windows build)

Three direct requirements and four indirect, and eight rows above, because two
of them are reported at a finer grain than a module: `go-licenses` sees the
shellescape copy vendored inside go-keyring and the Windows subtree of
`golang.org/x/sys` as units of their own, since each carries its own licence
file. Each licence text is in the module's own source, which `go mod download`
fetches and `go.sum` pins, and the full text of all of them is in
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES so that it travels with a distribution that is one
executable and nothing else. `spacebar licenses` prints that file from inside
the binary.

`github.com/spf13/pflag` is a direct requirement without being a choice. It is
cobra's own flag package and was already linked; it moved out of the indirect
block when a test began importing it to walk the flag tree, and `go.mod` says
what is imported.

SPEC.md §3.1 permits three more, each named with the reason it earns its place,
and no others. A pull request that would add another has to argue for it. This
list grows as the milestones land; it does not grow otherwise.

This list is asserted rather than maintained by hand:
internal/lint/notice_test.go holds it to THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES, which is
generated across linux, darwin, and windows on both architectures. That
generation is the point. mousetrap is behind `//go:build windows`, so a scan
run on the maintainer's machine does not see it at all, and the Windows archive
would have shipped without its notice. That is exactly the failure this file
exists to prevent, arrived at by the route nobody checks.

No dependency ships a NOTICE file of its own, so Apache-2.0 §4(d) has nothing
further to propagate here. If one ever does, its NOTICE is reproduced in full
below this line, and the same test fails until it is.

Everything else this tool needs is the Go standard library. `make vuln` runs
govulncheck and fails closed, so a build that cannot be checked is a build that
does not pass.
